We have the equations \(x+y = 4\) and \(3xy = 4\). Solving for \(y\) gives \(y = 4 - x\). Substituting into the product yields  
\(3x(4 - x) = 4 \Rightarrow 12x - 3x^2 = 4 \Rightarrow 3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0\).  

The quadratic formula gives  
\(x = \frac{12 \pm \sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4\cdot3\cdot4}}{2\cdot3}
   = \frac{12 \pm \sqrt{144 - 48}}{6}
   = \frac{12 \pm \sqrt{96}}{6}
   = \frac{12 \pm 4\sqrt{6}}{6}
   = \frac{6 \pm 2\sqrt{6}}{3}
   = \frac{6}{3} \pm \frac{2\sqrt{6}}{3}
   = 2 \pm \frac{2\sqrt{6}}{3}\).

Thus the solutions are \(x = \frac{6 \pm 2\sqrt{6}}{3}\), which matches the form \(\frac{a \pm b\sqrt{c}}{d}\) with \(a=6\), \(b=2\), \(c=6\), \(d=3\).  
The required sum is \(a+b+c+d = 6+2+6+3 = 17\).
